    'Richmond is a short run up the 580 and the 80 from our Oakland base, and it is the one city we cover that is heavily industrial and genuinely coastal at the same time. The refinery, the Port, the rail yards and the truck traffic along Richmond Parkway sit on one side of town. Miles of shoreline sit on the other. <strong>Both leave deposits on your paint that washing does not remove</strong>, and together they are why Richmond cars need real decontamination more often than cars a few miles inland.',
    'The industrial side puts iron in the air: microscopic metal particles that settle on horizontal panels, embed in the clear coat and then oxidize in place. You see them as tiny rust-colored specks, most obvious on white and silver paint, and you feel them as a gritty, sandpaper texture on a panel that looks clean. Soap does not lift them, because by then they are in the paint rather than on it. Removing them takes a chemical iron remover followed by a clay bar, which is exactly what our <a href="/exterior-auto-detailing-oakland-california/">Clay &amp; Seal exterior</a> is built around.',
    'The shoreline side adds salt. Marina Bay, Point Richmond, Brickyard Cove and the marina all sit in moving salt air, and salt accelerates corrosion wherever it settles and stays: lower panels, wheel arches, brake components and unprotected trim. Rinsing helps for a week. A sealant or a <a href="/ceramic-coating/">ceramic coating</a> is what stops salt from sitting on bare clear coat for months at a time.',
